  "blocks": [
   {
    "id": "p1",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "“There’s nothing to discuss,” barked my husband when I told him that Rosie, our 17-year-old daughter, had asked whether she could go to Kavos, Greece, for a holiday with her friends. “Of course she can’t. She’s too young to travel without the "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "presence",
      "ref": "v-presence"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": " of an adult. It’s completely "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "out of the question",
      "ref": "v-out-of-the-question"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ".”"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p2",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "So how come I found myself standing, two weeks later, at Gatwick Airport? "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "Tearfully",
      "ref": "v-tearfully"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ", I watched our eldest child disappear through the security checkpoint. In four hours, she would be in Kavos. It’s 2,500 kilometres away from England!"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p3",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "There comes a time when you have to let your baby go—for many parents, it’s around the age of 17. We know that there is likely to be something wilder than hanging around by the pool and building sandcastles, but the time has come to trust that they’ll look after themselves."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p4",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "But I still felt worried. Rosie tried to put my mind at rest. She said she and her friends would always look after one another and that it really, really was all going to be okay."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p5",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "After we had waved our last goodbye at the airport, I looked back. She was, to my surprise, looking back at me, too. Teenagers want to cut free, but it’s a frightening business for them, too."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p6",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "We made Rosie promise to text as soon as she had arrived in Kavos and at least once a day after that. Around 9 a.m. on the first morning, her first message came through. It had been twelve hours since I had left her at Gatwick. And all I got was: “I’m here.”"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p7",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "I wish there were safer places than these so-called teen "
     },
     {
      "t": "vocab",
      "v": "destinations",
      "ref": "v-destination"
     },
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": ". But like all the dangers that life can hold for them, at some stage, all you can do is warn them, warn them again, warn them once more, and then hope that some of your messages have gotten through."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "p8",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "content": [
     {
      "t": "text",
      "v": "My daughter got home safely. So did her friends. But Rosie is now talking about a similar journey next year. And my alarm bells have already started ringing."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
